    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The battle was the first American offensive in the Central Pacific and the first time in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> that a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> amphibious landing met serious, well prepared Japanese opposition.</li><li>A neap tide left only about 3 feet of water over the reef, so most landing craft could not cross it and the Marines had to wade ashore or be carried in by tracked LVTs, half of which were knocked out on the first day.</li><li>Of the 3,636 Japanese in the garrison, only one officer and sixteen enlisted men survived, and only 129 of the 1,200 Korean labourers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Marine cameramen filmed the dead on the beaches at Tarawa, and the decision on whether to release the footage was deferred to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>, who approved it.</p></aside>
    </section>

