    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">August 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Colonel Evans Carlson's raid on Makin Island makes the Japanese command aware of the vulnerability of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1943</span><span class="rf-text">The 6th Yokosuka Special Naval Landing Force reinforces Betio under Rear Admiral Tomonari</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1943</span><span class="rf-text">The Joint Chiefs of Staff direct Admiral Nimitz to plan an offensive in the Gilberts, and Rear Admiral Shibazaki relieves Tomonari</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1943</span><span class="rf-text">Marines land on the north shore of Betio under fire, with the tide too low for most landing craft to cross the reef</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1943</span><span class="rf-text">A Japanese counterattack at night and in the early hours is broken up, and Betio is declared secure on 23 November</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1943</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Times editorial warns that future assaults may cost even more, after public protest at the losses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early 1944</span><span class="rf-text">The 2nd Marine Division is withdrawn, and Nimitz launches the Marshalls campaign ten weeks after Tarawa</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-tarawa" data-v="731b3fd496" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The warships bombard Betio</b> Warships and aircraft shelled the island for about three hours before the landing. The Marines waited in the lagoon to land on the north beaches, while the defenders sat in their pillboxes and firing pits.</li><li><b>The landing is pinned down at the sea wall</b> The tide was too low for the Higgins boats, and only the tracked LVTs crossed the reef. They could not clear the sea wall, so the first waves were pinned against it. Defenders from the south beaches were shifted to the north.</li><li><b>By nightfall the line is half-way across</b> The arrival of tanks started the line moving on Red 3 and the end of Red 2. By nightfall it was about half-way across the island, only a short distance from the main runway.</li><li><b>Ryan's Orphans clear the western end</b> Major Ryan gathered remnants of his company with Marines and sailors from other waves and two Sherman tanks, and took them to a more lightly defended part of Green Beach. With naval gunfire they cleared the western end of the island.</li><li><b>The Marines cross the airstrip</b> Machine gun posts set up in the night had cut the Marines of Red 2 and Red 3 off from each other. By noon heavy machine guns put them out of action, and by early afternoon the Marines had crossed the airstrip to the south side.</li><li><b>Marines land on Bairiki</b> Word came that some defenders were crossing the sandbars to Bairiki. Portions of the 6th Marines landed there, with tanks and pack artillery, to seal off the retreat path. They found a single pillbox, which aircraft set on fire.</li><li><b>The 1st Battalion, 6th Marines attacks along the south coast</b> The 1st Battalion, 6th Marines, formed up after landing on Green Beach, attacked at 12:30 and pressed the Japanese east along the south coast. By late afternoon it had reached the eastern end of the airfield and joined the line from Red 3.</li><li><b>The Japanese counterattack is broken up</b> That night the Japanese formed up for a counterattack and sent small units to infiltrate the American lines. Concentrated artillery fire broke up the assembling force, and the full assault never took place.</li><li><b>The banzai charge at night</b> A large banzai charge was made at 03:00 and met with some success, killing 45 Americans and wounding 128. With support from the destroyers Schroeder and Sigsbee, the Marines killed 325 Japanese attackers.</li><li><b>The Marines attack both pockets</b> The 3rd Battalion, 6th Marines relieved the first battalion and attacked the eastern end. The 3rd Battalion, 2nd and the 1st Battalion, 8th closed on the pocket between Red 1 and Red 2 from west and east.</li><li><b>The island is declared secure</b> By noon the pocket between Red 1 and Red 2 had been reduced. The 3rd Battalion, 6th Marines reached the eastern tip by 13:00, and the island was declared secure at 13:30.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guadalcanal-campaign"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guadalcanal-campaign.jpg" alt="Guadalcanal campaign" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guadalcanal-campaign">Guadalcanal campaign</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marines_rest_in_the_field_on_Guadalcanal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> planned to take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a> to set up forward air bases that could support operations across the Central Pacific, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Naval doctrine of the time held that land-based aircraft were needed to weaken defences and protect an invasion force. The nearest islands able to support such an effort against the Marianas were the Marshalls, but a Japanese garrison and air base on the island of Betio, in Tarawa Atoll in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>, cut the Marshalls off from direct communications with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. American strategists therefore believed that the garrison and airfield on Tarawa would have to be neutralised first.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalcanal-campaign">Guadalcanal campaign</a> the 2nd Marine Division had been withdrawn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, where its losses were replaced and its men recovered from malaria and other illnesses. On 20 July 1943 the Joint Chiefs of Staff directed Admiral Chester W. Nimitz to prepare plans for an offensive in the Gilbert Islands. In August Admiral Raymond A. Spruance flew to New Zealand to meet the division's new commander, General Julian C. Smith, and begin planning the invasion with his commanders.</p><p>Colonel Evans Carlson's diversionary raid on Makin Island in August 1942 had made the Japanese command aware of the vulnerability and strategic significance of the Gilberts. The 6th Yokosuka Special Naval Landing Force reinforced Betio in February 1943, and the Japanese then worked for nearly a year to fortify the island.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tarawa&#8221; cites where it covers American strategic decisions, Japanese preparations:</p><ul><li>Derrick Wright, Tarawa 1943: The Turning of the Tide (Osprey, 2004)</li><li>Joseph H. Alexander, Across the Reef: The Marine assault of Tarawa (History and Museums Division United States Marine Corps, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Betio lies about 2,400 miles (3,900 km) south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>, at the southern end of the lagoon. It is a small, flat island about 2 miles (3.2 km) long and at most 800 yards (730 m) wide, with a reef about 500 metres wide around it and a long pier on the north shore. The garrison belonged to the Imperial Japanese Navy. The 3rd Special Base Defense Force had 1,112 men and the 7th Sasebo Special Naval Landing Force, under Commander Takeo Sugai, 1,497, with 14 Type 95 light tanks. The 1,247 men of the 111th Pioneers and 970 of the Fourth Fleet's construction battalion had been brought in to build the defences, and about 1,200 of these men were Korean labourers.</p><p>Rear Admiral Tomonari Saichiro, an engineer, designed the defences, which were meant to stop the attackers in the water or pin them on the beaches. Rear Admiral Keiji Shibazaki replaced him on 20 July 1943 and is quoted as saying that it would take one million men one hundred years to conquer Tarawa. There were 14 coastal defence guns, among them four 8-inch Vickers guns, about 500 pillboxes, forty artillery pieces and trenches linking every point. The command expected any attack to come from the open western or southern waters, since the guns were thought to make entry to the lagoon very difficult.</p><p>The American force was the largest yet assembled for a single operation in the Pacific: 17 aircraft carriers, 12 battleships, 8 heavy cruisers, 4 light cruisers, 66 destroyers and 36 transports, carrying the 2nd Marine Division and the Army's 27th Infantry Division, about 35,000 troops in all. The 2nd Marine Division, under Julian C. Smith, was to take Betio.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tarawa&#8221; cites where it covers American, Japanese:</p><ul><li>Derrick Wright, Tarawa 1943: The Turning of the Tide (Osprey, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before dawn on 20 November the island's 8-inch guns opened fire on the invasion fleet, and the battleships <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> replied. Three of the four guns were knocked out quickly, which left the approach to the lagoon open. After an air attack at 06:10 the naval bombardment continued for three hours. Planners had expected 5 feet (1.5 m) of water over the reef, but a neap tide left a mean depth of 3 feet (0.9 m). Major Frank Holland, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> liaison officer, had warned of at most 3 feet.</p><p>The attack began at 09:00, thirty minutes late. Only the tracked LVTs crossed the reef, and the Japanese who had survived the bombardment returned to their firing pits. Half the LVTs were out of action by the end of the day. Marines were pinned against the log sea wall or stuck on the reef some 500 yards (460 m) offshore. Colonel Shoup, wounded soon after landing at the pier, took command ashore. By nightfall Sherman tanks had helped push the line about half-way across the island. Rear Admiral Shibazaki was killed in the afternoon by a shell that hit his headquarters party. By the end of the day 1,500 of the 5,000 Marines put ashore were casualties.</p><p>On 21 November Marines took Green Beach at the western end and crossed the airstrip. On 22 November the 6th Marines reached the eastern end of the airfield, and that night a Japanese assembly for a counterattack was broken up by artillery fire. At 03:00 on 23 November a banzai charge killed 45 Americans and wounded 128, and the Marines killed 325 attackers with destroyer support. Betio was declared secure at 13:30.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tarawa&#8221; cites where it covers 20 November, 21 November, 22 November, 23 November:</p><ul><li>Joseph H. Alexander, Across the Reef: The Marine assault of Tarawa (History and Museums Division United States Marine Corps, 1993)</li><li>Derrick Wright, Tarawa 1943: The Turning of the Tide (Osprey, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The 2nd Battalion, 6th Marines cleared the other islands of the atoll by 28 November. Of the 3,636 Japanese in the garrison, only one officer and sixteen enlisted men survived. Of the 1,200 Korean labourers, 129 survived. In all 4,690 of the island's defenders were killed. The 2nd Marine Division lost 894 killed in action, 48 officers and 846 enlisted men, and a further 84 wounded died later. Another 2,188 men were wounded. Of the roughly 12,000 Marines of the division on Tarawa, 3,166 became casualties, nearly all in the 76 hours between the landing and the island being declared secure. Nearly 6,400 Japanese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> and Americans died in the battle.</p><p>The losses caused public protest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which could not understand such heavy casualties for a small and seemingly unimportant island. General Holland Smith, who had toured the beaches, likened them to Pickett's Charge at Gettysburg, and Nimitz received angry letters from the families of the dead. The new Marine Corps Commandant, Alexander Vandegrift, told Congress that "Tarawa was an assault from beginning to end." A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Times editorial of 27 December 1943 praised the Marines and warned that future assaults might cost more.</p><p>The 2nd Marine Division was shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and was completely withdrawn by early 1944, leaving the 2nd Battalion, 6th Marines to clear the battlefield of ordnance, guard the Seabees rebuilding the airstrip and help with the burials. Nimitz launched the Marshalls campaign ten weeks after Tarawa, and aircraft flown from the airfields at Betio and Abemama proved highly valuable. The division fought its next major landing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a> in June 1944.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tarawa&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Joseph H. Alexander, Across the Reef: The Marine assault of Tarawa (History and Museums Division United States Marine Corps, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from the American side above all. War correspondent Robert Sherrod wrote a report for Time magazine, published on 6 December 1943, and Staff Sergeant Norman Hatch and other Marine cameramen filmed the beaches and the dead. Holland Smith's autobiography gives his later judgement on the operation. The Japanese command was largely cut off from its units once its communication lines were destroyed, and its commander was killed on the first day, so the Japanese conduct of the fighting is told mostly through the American record and the defences themselves.</p><p>The remains of the dead have added to the record. In November 2013 the remains of one American and four Japanese were recovered from a site that was considered pristine, with remains as they fell. The remains of 36 Marines, among them First Lieutenant Alexander Bonnyman Jr., lay in a battlefield cemetery whose location was lost by the end of the war and found in March 2015. In March 2019 a mass grave of Marines, reportedly of the 6th Marine Regiment, was found on Tarawa.</p><p>Several matters are open to question. Counts of the garrison differ, 4,500 defenders or 3,636, with about 5,000 men under arms including construction troops. The number of Japanese killed by the bombardment, put at least 40 per cent of the defenders, is an estimate, as is the toll of a single tank shot in the final advance, which destroyed the bodies so completely that it was put only at 50 to 75 men. Above all, whether the battle was worth its price is disputed, as the legacy shows.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tarawa was the first time in the war that an American amphibious landing was opposed by well entrenched, determined defenders, and it was the most heavily defended atoll the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had invaded in the Pacific. Its significance for the Marshalls campaign lay in the lessons drawn. The casualties were put down to the miscalculation of the tide and the height of the reefs, the shortcomings of the landing craft, the inability of the naval bombardment to weaken a well entrenched enemy, and the difficulty of coordination between the services. Some three thousand shells were fired in the three hours before the landing, the heaviest bombardment of an invasion beach up to that time, but the high explosive shells usually went off before penetrating the defences. For the Marshalls the bombardments lasted a month and used armour-piercing shells, and the landing craft were armoured.</p><p>The failures at Tarawa were a major factor in founding the Underwater Demolition Teams, the precursor of the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> SEALs. Rear Admiral Turner directed the formation of nine teams, and thirty officers and 150 enlisted men were moved to the Waimanalo Amphibious Training Base to form UDT One and UDT Two.</p><p>Judgement on the battle differed. Holland Smith wrote after the war that the decision to seize Tarawa was a mistake and the casualties needless. Nimitz, Spruance, Julian Smith and Shoup disagreed, and Nimitz said that the capture of Tarawa knocked down the front door to the Japanese defences in the Central Pacific. Sherrod wrote that Tarawa had been given a name to stand beside Concord Bridge, the Alamo and Belleau Wood. More than one hundred of the Americans killed were never repatriated, and the Marines recovered in 2015 and 2019 were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 20 November 1943 the 2nd Marine Division landed on Betio in Tarawa Atoll and took it from its Japanese garrison in 76 hours. Nearly 6,400 Japanese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> and Americans died, and the lessons of the landing shaped later operations in the Pacific.</em></p>

